/** Translation maps for siman 052 — imported by _patch-siman-052.mjs */
export const TRANSLATIONS = {
  mechaber: {
    '1#main': `The law of a bird that fell into fire and change of appearance in organs. In it are 7 seifim. A bird that fell into fire and its intestinal children contracted, and the heart, gizzard, and liver — whose way is to be red — turned yellow, or the intestines — whose way is to be green — reddened: treifah, even in any amount of them that changed. And even if it did not reach the cavity — since they changed from fire burns, ultimately they will perforate. And the liver — the change does not forbid in it unless it changed opposite the intestines, meaning at the thin head of it and facing inward; or if it turned yellow opposite the bile; or opposite any place of its life-source. But the lung — we are not concerned for it because its ribs protect it. {Rama: And a spleen that turned yellow in its support — even only something — treifah, because ultimately it will perforate (Beit Yosef, in Rashba's view).}`,
    '2#main': `This — that change disqualifies in these organs — is specifically when they remained in their changed state after scalding them; but if they were scalded and returned to their appearance — kosher.`,
    '3#main': `If these organs did not change, and it happened that they were scalded and changed — treifah, since it fell into fire.`,
    '4#main': `Vegetation does not disqualify in the liver of a domestic chicken, for among its species are desert birds, and all desert birds' livers are green like grass.`,
    '5#main': `It fell into fire and it is not known whether its intestinal children contracted — it requires inspection; and if it is not before us to inspect — forbidden.`,
    '6#main': `A change was found in one of these organs even though it is not known to us that it fell into fire — treifah. {Rama: And there are those who permit as long as we do not know that it fell into fire, but we hang the change on something else (Ran and Mordechai in the name of Ravyah, Rashi, Maharai in Sha'ar 32, Rosh, and so on); and one may rely on them.}`,
    '7#main': `Treifot of change of these organs disqualify only in a bird; but in an animal, change of these organs does not disqualify even if we know it fell into fire. {Rama: And there are those who forbid also in an animal when it is known that it fell into fire and its intestines changed; and so is the custom (Mordechai and Hagahot Maimoniot in the name of several great ones, Rashba, and Or Zarua).}`,
  },
  'siftei-kohen': {
    '1#א': `["<b>And they turned yellow, etc.</b> But if they reddened — even if they increased in redness — kosher; and likewise intestines whose way is to be green — if they increased in greenness — kosher. Ran:"]`,
    '1#ב': `["<b>Like grass.</b> But green as an egg (which is called gogel) is kosher in the liver, for the liver is green as an egg if it is fatty — Tur in the name of Rashba; and it appears that therefore even in a thin bird it is kosher, and so it appears from the Mechaber's words. But Maharshal in Piskei Halachot siman 96 wrote that in a thin bird it is forbidden; and it also appears from the Mechaber's words that the same applies to heart and gizzard — we do not declare treifah except for green like grass, as Ran brought in Beit Yosef in the name of Ra'ah. But Rashba in Torat HaBayit daf 51b wrote that in heart and gizzard even like the appearance of an egg is treifah, and so wrote Bach and so Maharshal there:"]`,
    '1#ג': `["<b>Opposite the intestines.</b> Even in any amount — certainly they were scalded, also the intestinal children; and even if they are before us and we see no defect in them — treifah, end of Tur:"]`,
    '1#ד': `["<b>And facing inward.</b> And its one head above — which is not opposite the intestines — and that is the thick head in it, the place where it hangs from it; and likewise the outer side that faces the ribs is not opposite the intestines; and even if it is found green on that side at its thin head — kosher, end of Rashba:"]`,
    '1#ה': `["<b>Opposite the bile.</b> For we think the force of the fire that ruled and turned yellow as if the bile was perforated. Rashba:"]`,
    '1#ו': `["<b>Or opposite any place of its life-source.</b> That is, that it turned yellow near the place of its life-source so that if the place of vegetation is removed, an olive-bulk will not remain at the place of its life-source; and so too Ran and Beit Yosef, and see above siman 41 s.k. 3:"]`,
    '1#ז': `["<b>We are not concerned for it, etc.</b> It implies even if it fell into fire and changed in appearance we hang the change on something else — Rosh and Rambam and other poskim. However, there are poskim who are stringent where it changed in appearance from falling into fire, and Bach is stringent even in great loss, and it is not clear; and perhaps there is a scribal error in his words, and so his language somewhat implies — see there:"]`,
    '1#ח': `["<b>Because its ribs protect it.</b> For the ribs of a bird do not lie on their breadth but on their edge, and their breadth protrudes inward, and the lung is hidden among them. Rashi:"]`,
    '1#ט': `["<b>And a spleen that turned yellow in its support.</b> An animal — treifah; such is Rashba's language, and Beit Yosef brings it; and it implies but in a bird kosher, as Rashba said and the Mechaber above siman 43, that perforation does not disqualify in a bird's spleen; and therefore the Mechaber omitted this law, because he wrote in seif 7 that treifot of falling into fire do not apply to an animal:"]`,
    '2#_': `After scalding them. A little and one massages them, end of Rambam chapter 7 of Hilchot Shechitah; and Bach wrote that he holds that in greatly cooked, like the usual scalding in the Gemara, the change is not evidence to forbid nor is return evidence to permit, and so we rule like Rambam, for there is no one who disagrees with him, end of his words. And it requires further study whether one may rely on this leniently, since all poskim did not distinguish in this; and all the more so for us, who are not expert between scalding and cooking, as below siman 73 s.k. 8:`,
    '3#א': `["<b>And it happened that they were scalded, etc.</b> Nevertheless it is permitted to eat them roasted, and we are not concerned if they were scalded that perhaps they would change — Tur and Gemara and all poskim; and so Or Zarua beginning of general rule 54: even if it is known that it fell into fire, one need not scald them lest they change, for we do not establish a prohibition, end of his words; and it is simple. And in the gloss of Or Zarua it is written in the name of Rambam that where it is known that it fell into fire one must scald them, and this is not found in Rambam's words:"]`,
    '3#ב': `["<b>Since it fell into fire.</b> And even though in seif 6 we declare treifah if a change is found even though it did not fall into fire — this case is different, since the change is not seen except through scalding; and so he distinguishes in Rashba's novellae daf 60a; and so it appears in Beit Yosef, that in one law he wrote that Rashba wondered at Rambam who declares treifah when it did not fall into fire, and in another law in seif 6 he wrote that Rashba brought proof for his words and they agreed with him; and in this the question in Sela Chadasha daf 171a on the Mechaber was settled — see there:"]`,
    '4#_': `There are no vegetables, etc. Pri Chadash wrote that it deals specifically only if it did not fall into fire, and Bach permits in every matter, and so it appears, end of Shach:`,
    '6#א': `["<b>But we hang the change on something else.</b> For vegetation is always found in the liver from illness; and since we did not see that it fell into fire — why not hang it on illness, for we hang on a wolf, end of Ran; and so Ra'ah in Beit HaBahir daf 51b, and Rosh's words; and one should not hang intestinal change on falling into fire, for it is not common — for as soon as it falls into fire it flies out, and if its wings were burned so it cannot fly it burns in its place, etc.:"]`,
    '6#ב': `["<b>And one may rely on them.</b> In a place of great loss:"]`,
    '7#א': `["<b>But in an animal, etc.</b> Because its hide is hard, and also the thickness of the ribs protects it; and also intestinal children are hard to contract, for before they contract from falling into fire its hide and flesh burn, end of Tur in the name of Rosh; and he holds that we hang the change on something else:"]`,
    '7#ב': `["<b>When it is known that it fell, etc.</b> But when it is not known — kosher in an animal even not in a place of great loss:"]`,
    '7#ג': `["<b>And its intestines changed, etc.</b> It appears that therefore the Rav wrote briefly; and some say also in an animal, because he came to inform us that specifically when they changed before us do we practice prohibition in an animal; but if it fell into fire and is not before us we do not establish prohibition; and so is the essence, for Maharshal in Piskei Halachot siman 95 permits in such a case even in a bird; and furthermore, there are poskim who hold that change of appearance disqualifies also in an animal, and nevertheless they hold that inspection is not needed in an animal where it fell into fire, as Ran said and as Tur wrote in the name of Ba'al HaItur; however Bach wrote that inspection is needed in an animal, and that is l'chatchila where it is before us:"]`,
    '7#ד': `["<b>And so is the custom.</b> Pri To'ar wrote that this means not in a place of great loss. Or Zarua general rule 56 din 13: it implies we are not expert in inspection of falling into fire, and it requires further study why the acharonim did not write this from it, and perhaps it is included in "fallen." And Taz wrote that Rashba is also among those who forbid in this, and if the Mechaber had seen his words he certainly would not have been lenient against him, end of his words:"]`,
  },
  'turei-zahav': {
    '1#א': `And they contracted. Meaning they contracted — from the expression "their intestines contracted"; and some explain the language of heat — so too Mordechai:`,
    '1#ב': `And they turned yellow like grass. Rashba likewise wrote that there are two types of vegetation: one, like grass — meaning the color of grass; and the second, like an egg — meaning gogel. And Rashba said it is reasonable that the vegetation that disqualifies in the liver is like grass, for the liver is green as an egg if it is fatty, end of his words; and Maharshal wrote: if it is known that it is thin, one should not hang on fat, etc.:`,
    '1#ג': `And the liver — the change does not forbid in it except opposite the intestines. So wrote Rashba and Tur; and in the Gemara it is asked of Rav Yosef, son of R' Yehudah bar Levi, from R' Yehudah bar Levi: the liver turned yellow opposite the intestinal children — what is the law? He said to him: treifah. And it should not be except removed. Rava said: since it turned yellow opposite the intestines, it is known that it fell into fire and its intestines contracted; and Rashba holds that the force of the fire that ruled in the liver there already prevailed even in the organ beneath it; therefore we say it is known that the intestines beneath the liver also contracted, only they are not yet visible, for it is not their nature to be revealed as quickly as the liver; and it is not specifically opposite the intestines, but the same applies in other places — there is prohibition in yellowing, namely at the place of bile; for bile that was perforated is treifah; therefore here, since the heat of the fire ruled and makes the place of the liver as if perforated there, it also makes the bile as if perforated there; and if the yellowing is at the place of its life-source, where there is no prohibition from another organ that would disqualify through perforation, but the prohibition is from the liver that is as if that place of life-source was removed — therefore there is no treifah in this unless the yellowing is over the entire place of its life-source; but in part of it — namely, that something remained whole like an olive — there is no prohibition, for it is no worse than other perforation or removal, etc.; in all the liver except these places there is no prohibition in liver that turned yellow. But Mordechai wrote: liver that turned yellow opposite intestines — treifah, and the measure of its greenness is in any amount; and even though the liver was perforated — kosher — nevertheless, since the liver turned yellow we are concerned also for heart and intestines even though it is not yet evident in them; and furthermore one should be concerned that perhaps ultimately all of it will be removed; and from here there is support for four Geonim who declare treifah in perforation of the liver's courtyard, for we are concerned that ultimately the entire liver will be removed, since we declare treifah in liver yellowing at the measure of perforation of any amount that they forbade its beginning because of its end, end of his words. And Beit Yosef wondered: if so, why did they say specifically opposite the intestines? And he answered that perhaps we hold that ultimately all of it will not be removed, only specifically in yellowing opposite the intestines; and this matter is a great strain — as if Mordechai was told this thing, that the reason is because ultimately all of it will be removed; and from this one must enter a strain that they know specifically opposite the intestines; and he also brings support for the Geonim, and there is no hint or allusion in the Talmud, for they forbade only because of intestinal children alone. And it appears to me that Mordechai holds there are two reasons for treifah here: one, that since we see a defect in the yellowing, certainly other organs near it were also scalded, even though not yet visible — nevertheless it is possible that their nature is not revealed as quickly as this scalded place; and the second reason, that even in that organ itself that was scalded in part, and we see that the remaining part was not scalded — since in this organ itself whose nature is to change and be revealed, and nevertheless it was revealed only in part — this teaches that in another place in that organ it was not yet scalded; nevertheless we are concerned that perhaps afterward it will be scalded, for this blow will cause it; and he learned this from Rava's words, who said it is known that it fell into fire — for why did he need to say this; he should have said briefly it is known the intestines contracted, for the questioners also asked about liver that fell into fire, that it is known; otherwise yellowing does not disqualify at all, as Mordechai himself wrote there; rather Rava means there are two prohibitions: one, it is known it fell into fire and forbidden because this will harm it even in a place where the blow has not yet ruled; and another reason, that the intestines already contracted since they are near it, only it is not their nature to be revealed as quickly as the liver. It turns out that for the first reason there is prohibition also from the liver, for we forbid it because of its end; and Mordechai's words are explained from what is stated in the Gemara according to this; and according to this there is prohibition in liver yellowing in every place from the first reason in Rava's words; and for practical law we have only the ruling of Shulchan Aruch, that we do not say at all that ultimately all of it will be removed, but we say only Mordechai's last reason alone, and that is Rashba's and Tur's view above, for the Gemara's language proves as their words, from the challenge "and it should not be except removed" — this teaches that from the liver there is no prohibition; and so Ran; and therefore there is prohibition only at the place of bile and opposite intestines even in any amount, and opposite the place of its life-source specifically in all of it, as Beit Yosef emended Tur's words "life-source," etc.; and from this is proof for what is written in siman 41 seif 2, that liver that melted not at the place of life-source and place of bile — kosher, and we do not say cast the spoon in all of it and ultimately all of it will be scalded; for even in falling into fire here, which is explicit treifah in the Talmud, we conclude the halacha here that there is no prohibition because ultimately all of it will be scalded — a fortiori there, which is doubtful treifah; and even according to Mordechai who forbids here in liver yellowing in every place because ultimately all of it will be scalded — one does not compare treifot to each other, for there is a great difference between a blow that comes through fire and other illnesses, for we did not find in the Gemara concern in them, as it appears to me:`,
    '1#ד': `And facing inward. Meaning: but the outer side that is opposite the ribs — even if it turned yellow there — there is no concern for intestines, since there is a separation between the liver's yellowing and them; and according to Mordechai above, treifah even on the outer side from the reason that ultimately all the liver will be scalded — only we do not hold like him:`,
    '1#ה': `We are not concerned for it. In Tur it is written in the name of Rashba: but if we see that it reddened, etc.; and Beit Yosef wrote that it should read that it turned yellow; and likewise in what he wrote afterward "red" should read green, and he did not write a reason for this; and it is simple that his reason is because Rashba and Ran wrote "perhaps it turned yellow" — kosher, etc., as Beit Yosef copied; and therefore Beit Yosef also wrote that Rosh holds like those who disagree, whom we mentioned. And there is great room to wonder here on Tur and Beit Yosef, for it is stated in Torat HaBayit HaAruch daf 51: I found among some commentators that since they did not count lung together with heart, gizzard, and liver, it implies specifically these; but lung — even if it turned yellow — kosher, it is mere smoke that entered it; and what we say "there is no lung for a bird to contract" — even if it turned yellow he speaks; and to say that vegetation does not disqualify in it at all is not clear in my eyes; on the contrary, if in heart they said thus, etc.; and what is stated "there is no lung for a bird to contract" — they stated the reason: since ribs protect it; and if we saw that it was crushed — how did they protect it? Rather they spoke only from the default that inspection is not needed, etc.; and they spoke in these only because they are common; but a bird's lung is not common to contract and inspection is not needed; but heart, liver, and gizzard need inspection from the default; and if the lung is found crushed or contracted — treifah. I still have to say that it is impossible to count lung with them, because vegetation includes two colors: green like grass, which is grass, and green like an egg, which is gogel; and if it turned yellow like an egg — even if it did not fall into fire — treifah; and if it turned yellow like grass — kosher, from R' Nasan; and it is common in lung; and whatever can be hung on a derivative we hang and are lenient, for most animals are kosher; and this supports the explanation I wrote above to support the Geonim's words; and this supports what I wrote there: and lung of a bird — when they said it is not to contract, this means inspection is not needed lest green like an egg be found; behold animal lung needs inspection, end of his words. Behold before us that for Rashba, in green grass there is no concern even if it fell into fire; and certainly one should not say this deals only when we did not see that it fell, but certainly we are concerned even in green grass, for he wrote above on that page: when heart, gizzard, and liver turned yellow — evidence for the Geonim who hold that even when it is not known that they fell into fire we are concerned in whatever we see that turned yellow; for if not, you would explain to hang the yellowing on another change even in falling — behold majority is from the Torah and most animals are kosher, and we hang vegetation on something else; rather it must be that all vegetation of heart, gizzard, liver, and redness of intestines is a distant matter not through fire, and therefore we do not hang except on what is common; and since so, even when we did not see that it fell into fire it is so, etc.; if so, for him there is no distinction between fell or did not fall in any vegetation; you could challenge: in grass vegetation there is a distinction; in gogel vegetation too one could distinguish; if so, we have no proof for the Geonim that they do not distinguish; and furthermore Rashba wrote at the end of his words that I copied: and lung of a bird — when they said it is not to contract, this means inspection is not needed lest green like an egg be found — it implies grass does not harm at all; and this deals when it certainly fell — for that which "there is no lung for a bird to contract" deals when it certainly fell, for he said there is no lung for a bird — not to fall and not to contract, meaning it fell from a height of ten handbreadths or fell into fire — we are not concerned for its lung, for ribs protect it from the blow of the fall. And further proof: Rashba's main dispute is from the force of what they said: since ribs protect it, and whatever we saw contracted — how did they protect it? And if it deals with grass vegetation, how is it possible to say we saw it contracted — behold grass vegetation is in any case kosher from R' Nasan without our seeing that it fell; if so, what proof do you have in falling to say you saw it contracted; on the contrary, hang it from R' Nasan. Rather it is simple that grass vegetation does not disqualify lung at all for Rashba, even in falling, certainly; and gogel vegetation disqualifies even when we did not see that it fell; if so, why does Rashba disagree with those who disagree? It must be said the disagreeing ones hold that even appearance of gogel is kosher even in certain falling; and even though it is stated in Piskei Halachot: green (like liver) treifah — that is in an animal, where they say it is a scald; but not in a bird, since ribs protect it no scald rules over it; and so the language of the disagreeing ones implies: "there is no lung for a bird," etc. — vegetation does not disqualify in it at all — implying all vegetation does not disqualify in it; and in this Rashba disagrees with them, that gogel vegetation disqualifies also in bird lung, even if we did not see that it fell; and since so, it is difficult what Beit Yosef emended in Tur "turned yellow," that Rashba disagrees with Rosh in its greenness, and Rosh is the disagreeing ones of Rashba — this is not so, for the disagreeing ones permit even in gogel, as we proved; and for Rosh it is obvious it is forbidden in appearance of gogel even when we did not see that it fell, for Tur did not distinguish above in disqualifying appearances in lung between animal and bird; and therefore what Rosh permits here is in appearance of grass, that we do not hang on contraction; if so, there is no dispute here, for Rashba also concedes grass is kosher, as we proved, etc.; and certainly this emendation is not correct in Tur; rather Tur's words are also difficult in what he wrote dispute in lung reddening, that appearance of redness is kosher like appearance of grass, even in complete redness; if so, for Rashba too it is kosher, for he did not forbid except appearance of gogel; and if I were not afraid I would say Tur wrote his words thus because he did not see Torat HaBayit HaAruch, where it is further written "I still have to say," etc., and he thought Rashba forbids also in appearance of grass and called it contracted, certainly; but in truth it is not so; and I can reconcile Tur's words another way: namely, when we see a clear sign that a change in lung was done now, such as there is contraction in another place and there the lung reddened from it — in this for Rashba treifah; and therefore Rashba properly said: whatever we saw contracted, we do not hang the redness on something else; and for Rosh also in this kosher, since ribs protect; and therefore Tur wrote here the language of reddening, implying through another matter that caused this redness, as I explained to reconcile his words; and still my mind is not at ease with these words as fitting, and this is one of the places in Tur that commentators of Tur's words did not notice at all. Tur wrote: behold even if all of it was removed — kosher; meaning, and an olive-bulk remained at the place of bile and place of life-source:`,
    '1#ו': `That ultimately it will perforate. This is Rashba's view; and even though we already wrote that for Rashba we do not judge because of its end — for otherwise he should have declared treifah in every place in the liver — nevertheless he properly said here that ultimately it will perforate, because it was already scalded — all its supports around the blow were scalded and it is treifah from the reason that whatever was scalded ultimately perforates; but what was not scalded we do not say will eventually be scalded:`,
    '3#_': `And it happened that they were scalded. Meaning: by right one need not scald them in order to declare treifah if a change is found, for we do not establish a defect when there is no defect before us now; therefore one may eat them roasted:`,
    '4#א': `Vegetation does not disqualify in liver, etc. Specifically in liver they said thus, but not in heart and gizzard — so in Torat HaBayit:`,
    '4#ב': `Of domestic chicken. This is Rashba's view in Tur; and he learned thus from Rav Ashi, who thought to declare treifah in extra lobe, and they said to him: all desert animals are thus — so too here. And I wonder at this ruling, for in any case it is not compelled that it is green; rather, if green is found we can hang that it is not from change but that it is like a desert bird, even though the majority of domestic ones are not thus; for otherwise why hang the permission on desert birds? Rather certainly most domestic ones are not thus, only that one found was hung on desert birds; and if so, it is difficult: in the next seif he wrote that if it fell into fire and is not before us to inspect — forbidden — this teaches you have no permission in falling into fire until the permission is clarified for you, and not in doubt; and this domestic bird — since it is doubtful whether it is green by nature like desert birds, or was red and turned yellow from fire and is as lost, for it cannot be inspected and you cannot permit it until it is clearly permitted. Granted, if we had no prohibition except from finding yellowing in it, we could well say that for this reason it did not leave its permission and we hang the yellowing on its nature like desert birds; unlike here, where it fell into fire and is forbidden until the permission is clarified, and here it cannot be clarified. And if we say Rashba does not have that of seif 5, it would be fine, and so it appears from his words in the long Torat HaBayit daf 51, that he brought a version that seems to him change disqualifies only if it is known it fell into fire, but in an unspecified case we hang the change on something else — namely, there are those who permit whom Rama brings in seif 6; and he challenged him: if so, also in falling into fire we could say thus, for majority is from the Torah and most animals are kosher, etc.; rather we hang vegetation on what is common, and that is through fire, end of his words — behold he follows majority in falling into fire if there is something to hang leniently on, namely whenever vegetation is not before us; if lost, it is obvious it is kosher from majority; but if not, it is difficult on the author of Shulchan Aruch who brought both to halacha; and it is possible to say Rashba follows his reason that he holds even when we do not know it fell into fire it is forbidden if it turned yellow, as Shulchan Aruch wrote in seif 6; if so, we can say here it deals when it is not known it fell into fire, only we want to forbid it because of the yellowing — what is in its species of desert birds helps; but if not, he should have explained that it deals without falling, and not conceal that it implies falling into fire; and furthermore we already proved in seif 1 that Rashba permits grass appearance even in certain falling into fire; if so, the same applies to this permission of desert birds, for for him there is no difference between fell and did not fall in this. And on what Rashba wrote that this green of liver is like grass — whereas like an egg does not forbid, for liver is green as an egg if fatty — from this there is no doubt at all, for all are thus; unlike that matter where permission depends on desert birds, which is against the majority of domestic ones, as we proved shortly — and certainly difficult as written; and it is reasonable as I wrote initially that Rashba does not have that of seif 5; and if so, for us this permission of desert birds fell in a pit: if in falling into fire it does not help him, for it is forbidden without inspection, and here inspection is impossible; and if it did not fall into fire, there is no prohibition in yellowing at all, as Rama wrote in seif 6 that one may rely on those who permit, as it appears to me — correct; and afterward I found Maharshal, who wrote on this permission of desert birds: and it is not clear, for if so from where do we learn to be lenient in what the Talmud did not distinguish; and furthermore, since change of appearances does not disqualify except in falling into fire — if so, since there is a defect before us, why do we need to hang on natural change to be lenient and say perhaps domestic ones also changed, end of his words — it seems he intended what I wrote:`,
    '5#_': `And if it is not before us, etc. These are Ran's words; and Beit Yosef wrote his proof from what we say in the Gemara: there is no lung for a bird to contract; and that one certainly speaks in an unspecified case; for where it certainly contracted — behold it is before us that ribs did not protect it; but if it fell into fire — inspection is not needed, and specifically lung whose ribs protect it; but other intestinal children of a bird, for nothing protects them — need inspection, end of his words. And accordingly, according to what we hold, even if we saw that the lung turned yellow — kosher, as Tur wrote in the name of Rosh, and so is Beit Yosef's conclusion as I copied above in seif 1; if so, that which they said there is no lung for a bird is even if we saw that it turned yellow, and Ran's proof is not proof; or we do not hold like him in forbidding without inspection, and Shulchan Aruch should not have brought Ran's view here, since in Beit Yosef and Shulchan Aruch seif 1 he ruled like Rosh as I mentioned; only it is possible that even though Ran's proof is not proof, nevertheless it is reasonable to rule thus, since there is a defect here that it fell into fire — we say there is certainly change until it is clarified there is no change, as I will explain shortly — proof is not needed, and therefore Ran's words are halacha; and Maharshal disagreed with these words of Ran and brings proof from what we taught in the mishnah: fell into fire and intestinal children contracted — and it did not teach fell into fire alone, as it taught "trampled in a wall," etc.; rather certainly that there is no prohibition in falling alone but specifically when intestinal children certainly contracted; but in falling into fire alone there is not yet a defect before us. And it seems to me his words are wondrous in this, for there is one explanation of "and they contracted," namely the language of heat, as written in seif 1 in the name of Mordechai; if so, from where do we know the intestines were heated when there is no change of appearance? Therefore it must be said the matter depends on measure — namely, that it remained in fire long enough for the measure of heating; if so, his proof is not proof at all, for the mishnah means: fell into fire and intestinal children contracted — meaning it remained there for that measure; but certainly we do not see any sign of contraction, only presumably it fell into fire and remained there; and this certainly has no reason that as soon as it falls into fire it should be forbidden, even if they immediately removed it; but if it remained for the measure that it would heat — certainly there is a defect before us and inspection is needed; and in this matter it suffices to uphold Ran's words without another proof:`,
    '7#_': `And there are those who forbid, etc. Rashba is also among those who forbid, as I copied in seif 1 from Torat HaBayit HaAruch daf 51, who wrote: behold animal lung needs inspection; and Beit Yosef did not bring them; and certainly if he had brought them he would not have been lenient against him:`,
  },
  'baer-heitev': {
    '1#א': `Like grass. Meaning the color of grass; but green as an egg, called gogel, is kosher in the liver, for the liver is green as an egg if it is fatty — Tur in the name of Rashba. And Shach wrote: and it appears that therefore even in a thin bird it is kosher; but Maharshal forbids in a thin bird. And it also appears from the Mechaber's words that the same applies to heart and gizzard — we do not declare treifah except in green like grass; but Rashba wrote that in heart and gizzard even like the appearance of an egg is treifah, and so Bach and Maharshal; and if intestines whose way is to be red increased in redness, and likewise those whose way is to be green increased in greenness — kosher. Ran:`,
    '1#ב': `The intestines. Even in any amount — certainly they were scalded, also the intestinal children; and even if they are before us and we see no defect in them — treifah, end of Tur:`,
    '1#ג': `Facing inward. Rashba wrote: and its one head above, which is not opposite the intestines, and that is the thick head in it, the place where it hangs from it; and likewise the outer side that faces the ribs is not opposite the intestines; and even if it is found green on that side at its thin head — kosher, end of his words:`,
    '1#ד': `Its life-source. Meaning that it turned yellow near the place of its life-source so that if the place of vegetation is removed, an olive-bulk will not remain at the place of its life-source; and see above siman 41:`,
    '1#ה': `Protect it. (That the ribs of a bird lie on their edge and the lung is hidden in them.) Shach wrote: it implies even if it fell into fire and changed in appearance we hang the change on something else — Rosh and Rambam and other poskim. However, there are poskim who are stringent where it changed in appearance from falling into fire, and Bach is stringent even in great loss, and it is not clear, end of his words. (And for practical law one should be concerned like Ran and Rashi to declare treifah when it turned yellow like grass from fire, for it appears their words are that we see ribs did not protect them; however in great loss one may rely on Rambam and Rosh who permit. Pri Chadash.)`,
    '1#ו': `In its support. Shach wrote: Rashba's language is "an animal — treifah," and it implies but in a bird kosher, as the Mechaber wrote above siman 43 that perforation does not disqualify in a bird's spleen; and therefore the Mechaber omitted this law, because he wrote in seif 7 that treifot of falling into fire do not apply to an animal. (And Pri Chadash wrote both in animal and bird — see there.)`,
    '2#_': `That they scalded them. Shach wrote: this is Rambam's language — after scalding them a little and massaging them. And Bach wrote that he holds that in greatly cooked, like the usual scalding in the Gemara, the change is not evidence to forbid nor is return evidence to permit, and so we rule like Rambam, for there is no one who disagrees; and it requires further study whether one may rely leniently, since all poskim did not distinguish, and all the more so for us who are not expert between scalding and cooking, as below siman 73. (And by right one need not scald them.) An incident occurred in liver that was green like grass: they scalded it and it returned to its appearance outside, but inside it was still in change, and they permitted it since it did not fall into fire before us and its skin returned to the liver's appearance — the matter was revealed that from illness it turned yellow, and therefore beforehand it remained in its change; for if from fire, the essence of fire and its rule is from outside (R' Yehuda Bianu). (Pri Chadash.)`,
    '3#א': `And it happened. Shach wrote: nevertheless it is permitted to eat them roasted, and we are not concerned if they were scalded that perhaps they changed; and so all poskim, that scalding is not needed; and in the gloss of Or Zarua it is written in the name of Rambam that where it is known it fell into fire one must scald, and this is not found in Rambam's words. (But if it did not fall into fire before us, since beforehand the organs did not change and through scalding they changed, we hang leniently that from scalding they changed; and what the Mechaber wrote below in seif 6 to declare treifah when it is not known it fell into fire is when a change is found in organs not through scalding — Shach and Pri Chadash. Rikanti: a woman found vegetation in liver and did not know if it tended to redness; she also did not know if it turned yellow in the disqualifying place; she also did not scald it and the liver was lost — the chicken is kosher from the law of seif 6. Pri Chadash wrote on this: there is nothing in his words to rely on seif 6 to permit; and I already explained above siman 29 that whatever treifah is found and its matter is not known we do not hang leniently; and the same here — see there.)`,
    '3#ב': `Since it fell. Shach wrote: and even though in seif 6 we declare treifah if a change is found even though it did not fall into fire — this case is different, since the change is not seen except through scalding:`,
    '4#_': `Domestic. Pri Chadash wrote that this applies specifically if it did not fall into fire, and Bach permits in every matter, and so it appears, end of Shach. However, Torat HaBayit wrote specifically in liver they said thus, but not in heart and gizzard. (And Pri Chadash wrote like Pri Chadash, and one should not be lenient when it fell into fire — see Taz.)`,
    '5#_': `Forbidden. Everything requiring inspection that they did not manage to inspect — treifah from doubt (Pri Chadash). Taz challenged: behold this is Ran's view, and his proof from lung, who holds if there is change in falling into fire — treifah; and we say there is no lung for a bird to contract — meaning in an unspecified case inspection is not needed; but we do not hold thus, rather even if there is change it is kosher; if so, why did Shulchan Aruch rule here like Ran? And he answered: the Mechaber ruled thus on his own since there is a defect before us, and Ran's reasoning is not sound. An incident came before me: a chicken was in fire from great cold, entered to warm and was burned in skin and flesh at the ribs between body and collarbone, and a red mark was seen in circular skin like two fingers; they cut with a knife and blood was found coagulated inside, and I declared treifah since this needs inspection in intestinal children and we are not now expert in inspection; and all the more if a defect was made at the junction of sinews or in the signs or near where the lung lies that we declare treifah. Be'er Heitev — see there:`,
    '6#א': `We hang. And the reason is in Shach in the name of Ran: vegetation is always found in liver from illness, and falling into fire is not common:`,
    '6#ב': `One may rely. In a place of great loss. Shach:`,
    '7#_': `Custom. Pri To'ar wrote this means not in a place of great loss. And Shach wrote regarding an animal: if it fell into fire and is not before us we do not establish prohibition, for Maharshal permits even in a bird in such a case; however Bach wrote that inspection is needed l'chatchila in an animal where it is before us. Or Zarua: we are not expert in inspection of falling into fire; and Shach wrote it requires further study why acharonim did not write this, and perhaps it is included in "fallen." And Taz wrote Rashba is also among those who forbid, and if the Mechaber had seen his words he would not have been lenient against him, end of his words:`,
  },
  'beer-hagolah': {
    '1#א': `Mishnah Chullin daf 56:`,
    '1#ב': `From the incident of the chicken of R' Yehoshua ben Levi, and the baraita there:`,
    '1#ג': `There:`,
    '1#ד': `Statement of R' Yochanan, etc., there:`,
    '1#ה': `Rosh there and Rashba:`,
    '1#ו': `Question there, and it was resolved:`,
    '1#ז': `Tur:`,
    '1#ח': `There in the name of Rashba:`,
    '1#ט': `There in the Gemara daf 57, and like Chizkiyah, and as explained there in the Gemara:`,
    '2#_': `Statement of Rav Shmuel in the name of R' Chiyya there daf 56, and like Rosh's view, as Tur wrote:`,
    '3#_': `Statement of Rav Nachman bar Yitzchak there:`,
    '4#_': `Tur in the name of Rashba and Rabbeinu Yerucham:`,
    '5#_': `Ran there in his commentary in the name of Rabbenu Chananel, who cast a compromise on the matter:`,
    '6#_': `According to the Geonim' version and Rif in Rava's statement there daf 56; and so Rambam agreed in chapter 7, and Rashba in Torat HaBayit and Kol Bo, and Rabbeinu Yerucham:`,
    '7#_': `Tur in the name of his father Rosh, according to those who disagree, and Rambam in chapter 7; and so Ramban agreed (and Rabbeinu Yerucham and Maharshal):`,
  },
  'beur-hagra': {
    '1#א': `Like grass. Green as an egg is found in liver from fat. Torat HaBayit. And Ra'ah adds also gizzard, for fire turns yellow only like grass; and unspecified here is like Ra'ah:`,
    '1#ב': `And even if not, etc. In what is stated: liver opposite intestinal children:`,
    '1#ג': `That is, etc. Torat HaBayit and Tur:`,
    '1#ד': `Or if, etc. From comparing liver with heart and gizzard, which are disqualified because of themselves; and it must be thus:`,
    '1#ה': `Any place, etc. For it is not like drusah to rule from place to place; for otherwise there would be no challenge "and it should not be except removed," for if all of it were removed — treifah:`,
    '1#ו': `And spleen, etc. Like liver opposite bile, etc.:`,
    '2#_': `This, that change, etc. Gemara there; and the same for reds that turned yellow, etc.; rather the way of the matter is that it is usual to turn yellow through scalding; and all the more so thus, for certainly smoke entered them. Rosh (according to his version like the Rif's version there):`,
    '4#_': `There are no vegetables, etc. As written (Me'ilah 7a) regarding medium roses — all these; and therefore it is possible to find in domestic ones of its species as well:`,
    '5#_': `Fell, etc. From what is stated there 57a: there is no lung, etc., to contract; and if it contracted — behold it is before us that ribs did not protect it, only in an unspecified case (and Rosh disagrees on this); and specifically lung; and all the more so according to Ba'al HaItur, who wrote in seif 7 in the hagahah that falling into fire disqualifies also in an animal; only he mentioned bird since when it fell into fire we are concerned, unlike in an animal:`,
    '6#א': `Found, etc. As written there: since the liver turned yellow, etc., it is known it fell, etc.:`,
    '6#ב': `And there are, etc. According to their version that there is no text except "it is known that intestinal children contracted"; and even in Rif and Rambam's version, "it is known" refers to "contracted," and this means: it is known that since it fell, etc.; and proof from Rav Ashi: therefore do not eat, etc.; and therefore it is not in an unspecified case — for otherwise why would he be more concerned than all eighteen treifot? Rather it must be in falling into fire; and if the mishnah were unspecified, Rav Ashi would not need to say "unspecified." Ran. And Rosh proved his view that we are not concerned for lung even if it changed, and we hang the change on chance; all the more so in such a case; and he proved his view from that they did not count lung with heart, liver, and gizzard:`,
    '7#א': `There is no, etc. From that he counted it in a bird and not in an animal; and all these that he counted in a bird — Rosh there gives the reason; and therefore he did not count spleen, for perforation does not disqualify in a bird's spleen, as stated above siman 43; and therefore he omitted spleen law in Shulchan Aruch:`,
    '7#ב': `And there are those who forbid, etc. From what Levi taught: treifot that they counted, etc., beyond them, etc.; and there is no difficulty also in falling into fire; and that he mentioned bird is because it is common, like drusah and treifah, etc.; and in Tosefta it is taught also in an animal: its companion crushed it; and Ba'al HaItur wrote: in a bird it disqualifies from the default, unlike in an animal where we are not concerned from the default; and so unspecified here: when it is known, etc.:`,
  },
  kereti: {
    '1#א': `Reds, etc. Even if they increased in redness — it does not harm until they are green:`,
    '1#ב': `And they turned yellow. Shach wrote: Tur's view is that this green means green like grasses, which is called grass; but green like morika, called gogel, does not disqualify; and so Ran and Ra'ah; but Rashba in Torat HaBayit — as it appears his view is to disqualify in heart, etc., even appearance of gogel, except in liver since a fatty liver's way is to be like morika — it does not disqualify except green like grasses. And there is doubt in his words regarding thin liver like a board — if kosher like morika; and Pri Chadash wrote to be stringent, for he found support in Rambam's commentary on the mishnah; and see Peleti, where I agreed with Ra'ah, Ran, and Tur, for there is no proof from Rashba; and I also wrote there is no proof from Rambam in commentary on the mishnah — see there. And I also clarified Rashba's words in his novellae on Chullin, which need explanation at first glance:`,
    '1#ג': `Opposite intestines — even if there is no defect in intestinal children and they are before us — nevertheless treifah, since the liver turned yellow; and see Taz s.k. 3 what he wrote in Mordechai's name, that one should be concerned the entire liver will be removed; and see Peleti what I wrote in resolving Mordechai's words:`,
    '1#ד': `Lung — no concern, Rashba's view that we are not concerned; but if found — nevertheless treifah, for ribs did not protect it; and Taz challenged: either way — if green like grass it is kosher from Ran; if like morika, why need falling into fire — behold treifah from change of appearance; see Peleti what I wrote on this — correct resolution:`,
    '1#ה': `We are concerned; and if green like grass it implies kosher for all from Ran; and Nekudot HaKesef elaborated that Rashba retracted and holds since it fell into fire we do not hang on Ran; and see Peleti where I brought proof for this and wrote that Rashba did not forbid decisively, only in either-or — see there where I elaborated:`,
    '1#ו': `And spleen. That which was not counted in the Gemara with heart, gizzard, liver, spleen — this is proof for Rambam that perforation does not disqualify in a bird's spleen; and there in the Gemara everything deals with a bird:`,
    '1#ז': `That turned yellow, etc. See Peleti on the color of spleen:`,
    '3#_': `Since it fell into fire — meaning it is known and certain that it fell into fire. Pri Chadash:`,
    '4#_': `Of domestic chicken. Taz wondered — it is like lost lung, which we hold disqualifies; and see Peleti what I wrote on this and in resolving Nekudot HaKesef; and the essence is that it deals when it is not known it fell into fire, for then there is permission; and so Pri Chadash:`,
    '7#_': `When it is known it fell into fire and lung is before us — but if not before us we are not concerned at all:`,
  },
  peleti: {
    '1#א': `And they turned yellow like grass, etc. Shach wrote: from Tur's words it appears that in every heart, gizzard, and liver, appearance of gogel does not disqualify — only green; and so Ran in Ra'ah's name; but Rashba in Torat HaBayit — his view is to disqualify except in liver, and even there there is doubt if he means every liver or only fatty liver; and Pri Chadash wrote from Rambam's commentary on the mishnah it implies to be stringent. And I wonder, for I did not find in Rambam's words a place of prohibition, for he wrote in those organs whose way is to be green and like grass, and they judged from this that green means gogel like appearance of egg; and I say it is not so, for in change of appearance in lung Rambam wrote grass like kashuta, which is the growing herb; these five types are treifah, and grass like grass or green like tekhelet is kosher — behold the Gemara reads: only green — kosher means like grass; and Tosefta has a version thus; and Tosefta holds grass and grass are one; but Rambam explains on kashuta — grass, treifah; and grass — kosher; this teaches he holds to distinguish between grass and grass; therefore Rambam wrote these appearances whose way is green or like grass, and did not write like grass — this teaches he intended these two appearances, grass and grass, both green: grass means grass, and like grass means kashuta, both green and not gogel. In truth kashuta is a spice species called neglich, for Yerushalmi translates shechelet as kashuta, and Targum tophera is neglich; they tend to blackness, therefore called kashuta — language of Kush, black; and this is clear in Rambam's words; therefore there is no proof here to declare treifah; nevertheless one declares treifah when it is known it fell into fire; but if not known it fell, even Rashba did not say so, and one does not declare treifah except in green appearance similar to field weeds. And they are in Rashba's novellae chapter 1, on the passage "and R' Mani," etc.; he concludes at the end on that which lung was not counted among organs that fell: one could also say it is impossible to mix and change lung with them, because green includes two colors — one green like grass and herbs, and second like morika; disqualifying green in heart, gizzard, and liver is grass; whereas like morika — fatty liver's way is thus; and from mixing and teaching heart and gizzard, disqualifying green in heart and gizzard is like disqualifying green in liver, which is grass; but in lung, green like grass does not disqualify from R' Nasan; disqualifying green in lung is like morika, and it is even without fire-fall treifah, even if clarified it did not fall into fire, end of his words. One must understand his words: initially he brought that green stated in heart, gizzard, and liver all deals with green like grass similar to liver; if so, no difficulty why the mishnah did not teach lung — it deals with green like grass, and regarding lung kosher from R' Nasan; and now we must say like morika in any case disqualifies because of change of appearance. And conversely, since he concludes lung falling into fire does not apply at all — green like grass is kosher from R' Nasan, and like morika is disqualified in any case from change of appearance — why did Rashba need this, that green of heart, etc., is specifically grass similar to liver? It requires further study. It appears to reconcile: if we decide green is kosher from R' Nasan, we cannot say as Rashba that disqualifying green is grass similar to liver, for if so how is "there is no lung for a bird" understood — also in animal there is no lung to contract even if ribs do not protect, for scald of green from R' Nasan does not apply; and it must be said certainly even though the Gemara deals only with green like grass similar to liver, for practical law even in morika treifah; and it appears to say Rashba certainly holds this mishnah about greens stated in heart, gizzard, and liver was said generally in animal and bird — gizzard in bird, heart and liver which exist in animal and bird — both apply; one could well say green even like morika, for animal liver is not green like morika as experience and sense testify; but if he spoke only in bird, it must deal only with green like grass, for sometimes bird liver is like morika; and even though morika appearance also disqualifies in other organs, the Gemara spoke only of grass like liver; if so Rashba comes with either-or: if the Gemara deals in bird alone, morika green must be meant, for at bird liver morika is not change of appearance; and grass green must be meant; and it is difficult — green kosher from R' Nasan; but this is not difficult: how could one think like morika — without falling it is change of appearance. It is not difficult because of change of appearance — inspection is not needed, as Taz wrote, unlike from falling into fire where inspection is needed; therefore he rejected briefly that it is kosher from R' Nasan. If the Gemara deals in bird and animal, one could well say morika green; and in animal liver it is still impossible to teach lung, for that is change of appearance and inspection is needed, etc.; regarding animal everything needs inspection for any blemish and lung concern; Rashba answers either-or; from this it truly appears Rashba's view is also to disqualify morika green — only the Gemara did not deal with it; but Ra'ah and Ran imply they hold morika green does not disqualify, and Rashba alone disagrees, following his view that green is kosher from R' Nasan even in falling into fire; if so, vegetation disqualifies only grass; if so, what is "there is no lung for a bird" — ribs protect — even in animal kosher from R' Nasan; therefore morika green must disqualify; but in truth most authors disagree, as explained below in seif 2, that falling from R' Nasan does not apply; if so we say specifically similar to liver — only grass, as Ran and Ra'ah wrote; therefore the Mechaber ruled unspecified — correct. And see above siman 38, R' Ephraim's view on a blow in the wall — we do not say from R' Nasan; and this too is considered a blow since it fell into fire, and Rashba does not hold R' Eliezer's view. In essence I did not understand: is this from R' Nasan a matter unique to lung? We say regarding circumcision on the whole body that if blood was not absorbed it is green; if so, what of lung and what of heart, liver, or gizzard, and why do we declare treifah in them for green and not say in them too from R' Nasan? It requires further study:`,
    '1#ב': `And the liver, etc. Taz in s.k. 3 brought Mordechai, who wrote: even though the liver was perforated — kosher — nevertheless, since the liver turned yellow we are concerned also for heart and intestines even though not yet evident, and furthermore one should be concerned perhaps ultimately all will be removed; from here is support for Geonim who declare treifah in perforation of liver's courtyard, for we are concerned ultimately the entire liver will be removed, etc., end of his words. And Beit Yosef wondered: if so, why did they say liver turned yellow opposite intestinal children; and Taz also wondered. In truth it appears simple Mordechai wondered why they asked liver turned yellow opposite intestinal children and did not ask opposite bile or opposite any corner at place of life-source — where treifah is in truth; or he knew the Gemara: in all liver treifah, as we say greens stated in heart and liver — and it implies no distinction, only the Gemara should have asked liver turned yellow opposite intestinal children — meaning the inner side of liver opposite intestinal children, and intestinal children were not deficient at all, as Rashba elaborated; if so, the doubt falls: if it were a fire blow ruling, how is it possible the side opposite intestines, high side, outer side, and animal cavity where fire rules most — and soft intestinal children quickly absorb fire — would show no mark, while on the liver's inner side there is yellowing not from fire but from another accident or smoke that entered — that is the Gemara's question: liver turned yellow specifically opposite intestinal children; but certainly the baraita knew liver that turned yellow is treifah in those cases, etc.; and it is simple to him: nevertheless, since we see a defect here, we say the essence of fire does not rule in liver but in intestinal children, and smoke entered the liver and treifah; and all the more if there is scald in liver leaning toward the body where fire rules — it is obvious it is common and treifah, etc.:`,
    '1#ג': `The lung, etc. Tur wrote: and we say in the Gemara regarding lung we are not concerned for fire-fall because ribs protect; and Rashba wrote he speaks only regarding inspection, etc.; but if we saw it reddened (and Beit Yosef emended: turned yellow) — treifah, for we saw ribs did not protect; and Rosh wrote even if we see it red (and Beit Yosef's emendation: green) — nevertheless kosher, etc., end of his words; and the Rav Taz in s.k. 5 elaborated and copied Rashba in Torat HaBayit, who wrote to reject those who bring proof that falling-into-fire law does not apply to lung, from what is taught: reds that turned yellow — in which greens of heart, gizzard, and liver; and lung was not counted; and Rashba rejected: green includes grass and morika; grass in lung is by nature from R' Nasan; morika in falling is change of appearance and disqualified; if so, Taz holds on this Rosh does not disagree; if so, Rosh also concedes morika green disqualifies from change of appearance, and grass kosher, and Rashba concedes; if so, in what do they argue — this is the essence of his words. In truth, if for law there is an answer: as is clear in Gemara and poskim and Shulchan Aruch seif 3, if they did not change and it happened they were scalded and changed — treifah, because through scalding the defect was revealed; and this is only in falling into fire, as Shulchan Aruch explains — see Shach s.k. 12; but in mere change of appearance, change of appearance in lung was never devised to be concerned for at all; if so, one could say here is a great practical difference: if it happened that change is found only after scalding — for Rashba, who holds falling into fire applies even to animal lung, the defect was revealed and treifah; unlike Rosh, for fire does not rule at all in animal — what will you say, there is change of appearance? This is not so, for lung was inspected and there is no change, and after scalding we do not pay attention at all. And I add: if found after scalding — treifah; the same if found when lung is not inflated and cannot inflate, etc. — treifah, that it is only scalding and not inflation; unlike from change of appearance we do not pay attention, for our law in all lung appearances is after inflation, and we do not establish defect so much, and we do not see defect of change of appearance after inflation; if so, great practical difference, and let it be clear from all he argued. However, Shach in Nekudot HaKesef wrote Rashba retracted this explanation to say green is kosher from R' Nasan, since defect of falling into fire is before us we do not hang on R' Nasan; therefore in Torat HaKesef he did not bring this reasoning, and Tur followed, for Tur did not see Torat HaBayit; and he elaborated in Nekudot HaKesef, and the law is with him; and so Pri Chadash. And I think Rashba did not decide this for practical law to say even defect of falling before us we hang on R' Nasan, as I wrote above — only he spoke to reject those who claim falling law does not apply to lung from that they said heart, gizzard, intestines, and did not say lung; and Rashba answered on this; and certainly lung color is neither red nor green until Ran — red and green are change of appearance in lung; if so, in falling into fire, reddened or green — treifah, for there is fire-scald; if so, the Gemara said on the mishnah: if greens — disqualified; if reds — kosher — they did not say greens disqualified except in heart, gizzard, and liver; but reds kosher; if so, how consider lung among them — behold lung: even reds disqualified; and the mishnah teaches reds kosher; therefore lung is not included; and if it challenges the mishnah — why not count lung? One could also say the mishnah counts what whose way is to be red and changed to green; but in lung, which is declared treifah in every change of appearance, it does not count what reddened to green; and how consider what is declared treifah in every color and appearance; therefore it must be said there are those who hold nevertheless in lung reds are kosher from R' Nasan — only greens disqualified, namely morika; and on this Rashba properly argued either-or to your words: impossible to consider in grass immediately you are kosher, and in morika behold it is change of appearance, etc. And that is all for their words; but in truth Rashba also holds since there is scald, falling from R' Nasan does not apply; only Rav Beit Yosef did not grasp this, who emended emptying in Tur's words in place of reddening; and at first glance unnecessarily, for I already clarified even reddening disqualifies lung in fire-fall; but it seems to Rav Beit Yosef that nevertheless kosher from R' Nasan, and as Rashba wrote; therefore he emended turned yellow, meaning morika, etc. Summary for law: no practical difference in green like grass — kosher for all; and the same in reddening; but green like morika — it appears to be stringent; and so Pri Chadash — correct:`,
    '1#ד': `And spleen, etc. This is proof for Rambam that perforation does not disqualify in a bird's spleen; for otherwise it would be difficult: the Gemara counted heart, gizzard, liver — why not count spleen too, as it challenged regarding lung; therefore it must be as Rambam, that in a bird perforation does not disqualify in spleen, etc.; and the mishnah deals with a bird — full proof:`,
    '1#ה': `That turned yellow, etc. In truth Rashba wrote thus; but I did not know who told Rashba spleen color until it was said turned yellow — treifah; perhaps reddening is kosher; perhaps spleen color is truly green like morika as all see, and when reddened — treifah; and in truth in Chullin daf 111 we say: and this is in liver; but spleen is mere fat — it implies he thought blood was swallowed; if so, organ color tends to red; nevertheless it requires further study to be lenient, for we see a defect:`,
    '4#_': `In liver of domestic chicken. The Rav Taz in s.k. 9 wondered: granted Rashba holds when intestinal children are lost and inspection impossible — kosher; therefore here it is like lost, we do not know if domestic or desert; unlike according to what we hold in seif 5 that lost intestinal children — treifah; if so, since we do not know domestic or desert, it is like lost and treifah; and he properly wrote that also above, who is lenient in green from R' Nasan, also proves he holds lost — kosher; but if lost — treifah; then it is not comparable to change of appearance where lung is lost — by law kosher, for what we hold treifah is only rabbinic enactment of Chachamim, as Shach wrote above siman 39; therefore in doubt whether from scald or from R' Nasan we hang on R' Nasan; but here the doubt is whether to hang on R' Nasan or not — it is like lost and treifah; therefore it must be he holds lost kosher. Behold the Rav in Nekudot HaKesef explained: when we say treifah in lost — all intestinal children; but here some intestinal children are before us with no defect, only domestic liver has scald — we hang on desert birds; reasonable matter; but if not, even in lost one could say thus; if so, Ran, who is teacher of this law, would learn from lung whose ribs protect in either-or: if no defect — obviously kosher; if defect — we see ribs did not protect; therefore it must be they are not before us; and the reason ribs protect — otherwise treifah; and difficult: other organs heart, gizzard, liver — what is their benefit if not before us, according to his law treifah, for they are not before us to inspect; and if before us with no defect — still difficult why ribs protect, behold they are before us elsewhere with no defect; therefore it is clear Ran's view is not to distinguish. However, Taz himself wrote from Ran's words there is no proof that every difficulty where lung did not change — we see ribs did not protect; and Rosh already wrote we hang change on chance, and Shulchan Aruch ruled; if so, Ran's law does not exist, only one could say they ruled by reasoning since it fell into fire it deteriorated and therefore needs inspection; and especially according to seif 6 that change disqualifies without falling into fire; if so, falling into fire is unnecessary; and it must be falling into fire alone obligates inspection; and therefore division is fine as Shach wrote — examine well. Also one could say the Mechaber deals without falling into fire, and lost does not apply at all, etc.; and so one should rule; and so Pri Chadash:`,
  },
  'pitchei-teshuva': {
    '1#_': `That they scalded them. Be'er Heitev in the name of Pri Chadash; and see She'eilat Yaavetz part 2 siman 170, who wrote he permitted only where we did not know it fell into fire, as Pri Chadash's language implies in that incident; but where it certainly fell into fire one may not permit — see there:`,
    '2#_': `Also in an animal. See responsa of Radbaz HaChadashot siman 231, who wrote: if the heart turned yellow and did not reach the cavity — there is room to permit, even though it is written in seif 1 "and even if it did not reach the cavity," etc. — that is specifically in a bird, but not in an animal — see there:`,
    '3#_': `[See responsa Chatam Sofer siman 54, who wrote regarding bulls fed refuse of cooking, which is hot water and sometimes boiling, and many drank and died: if one should be concerned in an unspecified case for those bulls that intestinal children contracted from boiling water — this is certainly that intestinal children contracted; we say only specifically in fire; and what caused several animals to die from drinking boiling — if there were medicine for them they would live; and to add treifot — certainly. If before cooking we find change in inner organs that we declare treifah in them — any change we are concerned for in the meat that the doctor drags and we declare treifah in any case; but if the organs are fine or were not inspected at all, and after cooking we find change — one should not hang on contraction at all — see there:]`,
  },
  'mateh-yehonatan': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 52 in Shulchan Aruch seif 4) There are no vegetables that disqualify in liver of chicken, etc. Taz s.k. 9 — see there: his intent is to resolve the difficulty, for all this is according to Rashba, who holds if intestinal children are lost without inspection — kosher, unlike Ran's method, who declares treifah, as the Mechaber wrote in the next siman; if so, here too, even though among its species are desert birds, we should declare treifah, for it is no worse if lost — see there. And Shach in Nekudot HaKesef holds to answer: that which we hold if not before us to inspect — treifah means all intestinal children were lost; but if some intestinal children are before us without any recognizable defect, we hang leniently that also in those lost there was no defect; and here it deals where some intestinal children are here that did not change, only because of liver vegetation we would forbid; and since among its species are desert birds we hang leniently. And one could say on this: this law "if not before us" is from Ran, and his proof: in chapter 1 we say there is no lung for a bird — not to fall and not to contract; and it is difficult: if lung is before us with defect — treifah, for we see fire ruled; if no defect in it — also other intestinal children kosher even if we know it fell into fire; if not before us and such — if we know it certainly fell into fire — treifah; and according to Shach in Nekudot HaKesef, who holds wherever some intestinal children are before us without defect, even if part lost — kosher — still difficult from the beginning either-or: if other intestinal children are before us without defect, even if treifah in falling applies to lung, lung is also kosher, for lung is not before us and in other intestinal children there is no defect; if other intestinal children are also not before us — it must be treifah because of other intestinal children; and one could say: this teaches where lung is before us and other intestinal children are not before us and there is no defect, and lung is nevertheless treifah, for from lung there is no proof at all on other intestinal children, for there is no lung for a bird to fall and not to contract, since ribs protect them, unlike other intestinal children — one could say fire ruled in them; meaning Gemara language and all commentators imply he means where there is defect in lung we do not hang on fire at all and it is kosher; if so, one could say as written; and it must be Shach holds certainly where before us, one can establish whether fire ruled or not; then even where some are before us without defect — also treifah; only here, since among its species are desert birds, if impossible to establish on the matter whether for some intestinal children besides lung it is kosher — strained:`,
  },
  'kaf-hachayim': {
    '1#_': `(1) [Seif 1] A bird that fell into fire and its intestinal children contracted, etc. — they contracted from the fire and their appearance changed — language of "their intestines contracted." Rashi Chullin 56a. Pri Chadash ot 1; and some explain language of heat. Mordechai. Taz s.k. 1.`,
    '2#_': `(2) There. A drop that fell into fire, etc. — and the same if it fell into boiling water — it is as falling into fire. Rokeach siman 406. Turei Zahav ot 1; Shulchan Aruch HaRav ot 484 end ot 5; Mechaber ot 2 and ot 8; Shulchan Aruch HaMekutzar ot 1; Zivchei Tzedek ot 1; Anaf Yosef siman 34 ot 1; Toldot Adam siman 74 ot 2; Zivchei Tzaddik ot 2.`,
    '3#_': `(3) R' Shmuel Kluger was in doubt in Ashelei Ravravi on Pri Megadim at end of the book whether boiling water mentioned means specifically while still on the fire, but removed from the fire — not, etc.; and he left it requiring further study — see there; and for practical law it is reasonable: as long as it is yad soledes bo, and even if removed from the fire — also called boiling water, as Shulchan Aruch wrote below siman 105 seif 2 and in Orach Chayyim siman 318 seif 9; and it is also reasonable that even kli sheini and yad soledes bo is also called boiling water for this matter, as Pri Chadash wrote siman 68 ot 18 — see there. Zivchei Tzaddik ot 2.`,
    '4#_': `(4) There. And intestinal children contracted, etc. — and Bach wrote specifically when it fell into fire in a matter where it is possible they contracted or intestinal children were heated there — then if they changed — treifah; but where it is impossible they were heated or contracted in such a fall into fire — even if they changed — kosher. But Maharshal in Yesh Nochalin Piskei Halachot siman 92 wrote: even if intestinal children did not contract, only changed — treifah. And so Mechaber ot 9; Shulchan Aruch HaMekutzar ot 2; Zivchei Tzedek ot 2; Toldot Adam there ot 3; Zivchei Tzaddik ot 3.`,
    '5#_': `(5) And if it remained the measure of kav chananiah even though they did not contract and did not change — it needs inspection. Mechaber ot 10. Zivchei Tzedek there. Zivchei Tzaddik ot 4; and inspection means to scald them, as written below seif 2.`,
    '6#_': `(6) And if it did not remain the measure of kav chananiah and they changed — treifah. Mechaber ot 11. Zivchei Tzedek there. Anaf Yosef siman 34 ot 1. Zivchei Tzaddik ot 4.`,
    '7#_': `(7) And even if it did not turn yellow or redden, only the outer side alone of the intestinal children and not the inner parts — treifah. Taz above siman 38 s.k. 4. Knesset HaGedolah in Hagahot HaTaz ot 1; Shulchan Aruch HaMekutzar ot 3; Zivchei Tzaddik ot 6.`,
  },
  'yad-avraham': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 52 seif 7 in the hagahah) And there are those who forbid also in an animal, etc., and so is the custom. In Be'er Heitev he wrote: what is this pain, and so is custom in an uncommon matter. And one could say according to what Maharanch wrote in responsa Mayim Amukim siman 5: specifically if it is an incident we say there is no custom in an uncommon matter; but if done according to sages, leaders of the land — it is fixed practice. And with this one resolves what Noda B'Yehudah wrote in part 2 of Yevamot regarding father's wife: in Spain the custom is to permit, even though uncommon; but the intent is done according to sages. And so one resolves what Sdei Chemed wrote in Orach Chayyim siman 32: our custom that even in attachment letter to letter found in a Torah scroll on Shabbat or Yom Tov to take out another — uncommon — nevertheless custom applies, meaning as stated. If so, this hagahah here also intended this. And one could also say here what Be'er Heitev challenged on the hagahah below siman 567 seif 4 — see there:`,
  },
  'yad-ephraim': {
    '1#_': `Opposite any place of its life-source. Be'er Heitev; and see Mateh Yosef daf 58b in an incident where liver was found green initially like grass, and it was evident bile spilled into it that was not full as usual, and my teachers permitted saying bile spilled into it, etc. — see there; and Pri Chadash disagrees and forbids:`,
    '2#_': `And so is the custom. Be'er Heitev; and see Radbaz siman 231 regarding liver found green — see there:`,
  },
  'nekudot-hakesef': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 52 in Taz s.k. 5) He elaborated to wonder on Tur and Beit Yosef and wants to say green like grass — there is no one who disqualifies, for green like grass is kosher from R' Nasan without our seeing it fell; if so, in falling too we hang like R' Nasan, and there is no difficulty; and Tur and Beit Yosef's words are clear that Rashba answers initially: that which he counted heart, gizzard, and liver — not specifically these, but the same applies to lung if it contracted, and they spoke only regarding inspection; and for this change it is obvious green like grass disqualifies lung in falling into fire — for if green like grass were kosher, it is not possible to say "not specifically," but specifically they taught heart, gizzard, and liver, which are disqualified like grass, and lung kosher; and one cannot say it deals with gogel green, for it deals similar to heart, gizzard, and liver; and furthermore in gogel green one cannot say "not specifically," for gogel disqualifies lung even without falling into fire, and it is not relevant to mention here. Rather certainly when we say that which they said heart, gizzard, liver that turned yellow are disqualified — the same applies to lung that turned yellow, perforce like grass; and what Rashba said afterward "I still have to say it is impossible to count lung with them," etc., is another reason — specifically he counted heart, gizzard, and liver, and it is impossible to count lung with them because lung is kosher when green like grass; however for halacha Rashba does not hold this "I still have to say," rather as he explained initially; and so is proven from his words in the short Torat HaBayit, and as I will write shortly; and so Ran in chapter These Treifot explicitly — this is his language: that which lung was not counted with them — even though where it fell and turned yellow — treifah — for even though above we said green is kosher from R' Nasan, that is when it did not fall into fire; but in falling into fire we do not hang lung on R' Nasan, just as we do not hang liver on illness; this was the reason: because lung is not made to contract like heart, gizzard, and liver, since ribs protect it; and furthermore its law may not equal those, that heart, gizzard, and liver need inspection whenever it fell into fire, and bird lung does not need inspection; and Rashba wrote vegetation disqualifies in liver only like grass, etc., end of his words — this is Rashba's first answer; and if so, grass disqualifies lung in falling into fire; and so is proven explicitly from what he wrote in the short Torat HaBayit daf 23a: even though the Sages did not count lung among reds that turned yellow, it appears to me the same applies, and this is the reason — only because bird ribs protect it; and from the default inspection is not needed for lung even if it fell into fire before us; therefore they did not count it among those needing inspection after falling into fire; but if we saw lung turned yellow — treifah, for it is known ribs did not save it; and there are those who permit and their words were not clarified at all, end of his words — behold explicitly he does not hold halachically this "I still have to say," etc., rather like the first reason in the long Torat HaBayit; and if so, also gogel green he declares treifah, as Ran explicitly wrote; and therefore Tur properly aimed at Rashba's view and Rosh's view — Rosh obviously disagrees with Rashba, for Rosh wrote it implies even if lung changed we do not declare treifah, since ribs protect we hang change on something else, not on fire-fall, etc.; one cannot say since we see it changed it is treifah, for then lung should have been counted with heart, gizzard, and liver; rather certainly from not counting with them, change does not disqualify even in falling into fire, end of his words — all this is clear in my eyes, and I did not need to elaborate except because he elaborated to wonder on Tur and Beit Yosef and concluded this is one of the places commentators of Tur did not notice; in my humble opinion there is no wonder at all, and Tur and Beit Yosef's words are simple and clear, and commentators of Tur did well not to write anything here. I again saw in Rabbeinu Yerucham Netiv 15 chapter 5 ot 21 — it implies there explicitly also as I wrote, that for Rashba all green is treifah from the reason they said there is no lung for a bird to contract only regarding l'chatchila that inspection is not needed; but if we saw it contracted, even grass — treifah — see there. And further it appears to me: even if you wonder that Rashba declares treifah is specifically in gogel green, nevertheless let it be clear on Tur why he wrote Rosh disagrees — for it is proven from Rosh that even in gogel he permits, like the view of "there are those who interpret"; and if not, what does Rashba argue against "there are those who interpret" — perhaps they deal with grass, especially since it appears explicitly "there are those who interpret" are Tosafot who wrote thus on daf 26 top, and they did not mention morika green in their words, only unspecified — it implies they permit all green; if so, Rosh, who concludes thus, holds like them; and one should not challenge: if so, how does Rosh answer that which all treifot the Sages counted in animal — correspondingly in bird — this is not a difficulty, for Rashba already challenged "there are those who interpret," and as they answered, so Rosh answered. And further: let it be clear for their view they certainly were not unaware of that which all treifot the Sages counted in animal — correspondingly in bird; for regarding that very "there is no lung for a bird to contract" which "there are those who interpret" brought — it is so in the Gemara, version in chapter 1 daf 57a: what is "there is no lung for a bird"? If we say it has none at all — behold we see it has; rather it is not declared treifah; and Levi taught: treifot the Sages counted in animal — correspondingly in bird; rather it has no falling and contracting — what is the reason? Since most of its ribs protect it, etc.; rather it appears to me commentators certainly hold all eighteen treifot declared treifah from clear treifot such as perforated, deficient, removed, and the like — correspondingly in bird; but contracted, which is not certainly treifah except because ultimately it will perforate — in bird the Sages knew, since ribs protect it, even if it changed and contracted it will not ultimately perforate — so it appears clear in their view; and it appears clear Rashba also does not consider this a challenge on "there are those who interpret," for otherwise he should have included it in his other challenges; rather he did not write thus, only like a branch and proof for his words, meaning: since in animal it is declared treifah, correspondingly in bird it is declared treifah in them. But one may well say for "there are those who interpret"' view as I wrote — and it is clear; examine well. If so, it is also obvious that without falling into fire it is not declared treifah, for since in falling into fire when it became green it is kosher — all the more so without our seeing it fell into fire; and in animal, where green gogel is treifah even without falling into fire — perforce the Sages knew in animal in such a case it will ultimately perforate; and in animal certainly, since it is green in this appearance, lung certainly fell into fire; and even though Rosh holds we do not say when known it fell into fire and also contracted — this does not apply in animal regarding other intestinal children, but lung green gogel is scald, certainly from fire, or even if you say from something else — nevertheless the Sages knew it will ultimately perforate; and what Tur wrote unspecified above siman 38 that appearance of green gogel is disqualified and did not distinguish animal and bird — because he relied on what he wrote here in this siman that there is no lung for a bird in Rosh's name: even if it fell into fire and we saw it contracted — kosher; behold he explicitly excluded bird from animal regarding yellowing; and all the more so without falling into fire; so it appears clear in Tur's view; and therefore he wrote unspecified in Rashba's name: if we saw it is green — treifah, and Rosh disagrees and permits, and did not distinguish between green like grass and gogel, because for Rashba even grass is treifah, as I wrote above, and for Rosh even gogel is kosher. If so, what Taz elaborated here to wonder on Tur is not difficult at all, for two reasons: one, for Rashba even grass is treifah; and furthermore, for Rosh even gogel is kosher, as written — and it is clear and true; examine well:`,
    '2#_': `(Taz s.k. 9) He wondered on Rashba and Shulchan Aruch. It is not difficult at all, for this case is different, for it is common in desert birds; and we hang on common; and in any case it is not difficult, for here since there is no defect except in liver, the matter is proven that it is from desert birds, for otherwise there should have been some defect also in other organs; and it is not comparable to seif 5 where it is not before us. And this is clear:`,
    '3#_': `(Taz s.k. 10) He wrote on Maharshal that his words are wondrous and explained a strange meaning; but I wondered on Maharshal, for the mishnah teaches thus: fell into fire and intestinal children contracted — if greens — disqualified; if reds — kosher; trampled in a wall or animal crushed it and it convulses — if it remained from evening to morning and was slaughtered — kosher; if so, it should teach "and intestinal children contracted" because of reds kosher and also because of greens, for they are certainly disqualified; but when not before us it is only doubt, and it did not teach trampled in a wall, only to inform that if it remained overnight and was slaughtered — kosher. And I looked in Maharshal chapter 1 siman 92 and saw he wrote: otherwise why not teach fell into fire alone, as the mishnah taught at beginning of the chapter drusah alone and was not trampled and flesh reddened; rather certainly it is not relevant to teach because as soon as trampled it is treifah from doubt lest flesh reddened until inspected to remove it from treifah doubt, unlike falling into fire, end of his words — meaning lion and wolf drushah of chapter beginning, not as he understood that Maharshal meant trampled in a wall; nevertheless from this too it is not difficult, for there it needs to teach lion drushah in large animal and wolf in small animal and other tramplers, and dispute of R' Yehuda, unlike here where it needs to teach intestinal children contracted for the reason I explained; examine well:`,
    '4#_': `(Taz s.k. 11) He pressed his mishnah, for besides this Rashba mentioned there on the previous page the poskim's reasoning at length and Ba'al HaItur's reasoning and concludes; and also this is not clear, etc.; and the correct view is animal law equals bird, and here and there treifah, and here and there inspection is needed if it fell into fire; and Ran also wrote all these matters, and his view is like Rashba; Beit Yosef brings it and wrote such is Rashba's view, intending the above words of Rashba; if so, Beit Yosef saw more than he saw; nevertheless he did not rule there for the reason explained in Beit Yosef: since Rambam and Rosh intend one view, so we rule; and all the more Ramban holds like them, end of his words; and so Rabbeinu Yerucham Netiv 15 ot 10, and Maharshal chapter 1 siman 95:`,
  },
};




